14
00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.402
Why does the scope matter
as much as the policy?

15
00:00:52.402 --> 00:00:56.233
Because Conditional Access for
workload identities is narrow,

16
00:00:56.233 --> 00:01:01.003
as Microsoft Learn's Conditional Access
for workload identities page sets out.

17
00:01:01.003 --> 00:01:03.215
Block is the only grant control.

18
00:01:03.215 --> 00:01:08.384
It covers single-tenant service principals
registered in your tenant, and nothing

19
00:01:08.384 --> 00:01:13.403
else: not Microsoft or SaaS apps, not
multitenant apps, and not managed identities.

20
00:01:13.403 --> 00:01:17.977
A policy assigned to a group that holds a
service principal isn't enforced for it;

21
00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:20.889
you have to assign the
service principal directly.

22
00:01:20.889 --> 00:01:25.278
And Workload I D Premium is needed
to create or change these policies.

23
00:01:25.278 --> 00:01:29.891
So in this module you hold the
program's two policies, C A oh fifteen

24
00:01:29.891 --> 00:01:34.590
for location and C A oh thirty-seven
for high risk, as reviewed files.

25
00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:37.870
They're scoped by object
I D, one ring at a time,

26
00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.970
and every one is created report-only.

27
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.599
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

28
00:01:47.599 --> 00:01:50.282
which takes about sixty-five
minutes with the lab.

29
00:01:50.282 --> 00:01:54.393
You'll state the four limits of
Conditional Access for workload identities,

30
00:01:54.393 --> 00:01:56.807
and what covers the
identities outside them.

31
00:01:56.807 --> 00:01:59.535
You'll explain why each policy is rendered

32
00:01:59.535 --> 00:02:02.419
once per ring and always
created report-only.

33
00:02:02.419 --> 00:02:04.245
You'll run the pre-merge check,

34
00:02:04.245 --> 00:02:07.970
the tenant check and the plan,
and read what each one reports.

35
00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:12.532
You'll read a sign-in summary and say
whether a ring is ready for enforcement.

36
00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:15.783
And you'll see a ring held
without the allow-enforce switch,

37
00:02:15.783 --> 00:02:19.033
enforced with it, and held again
when the summary is edited.

38
00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:23.435
Before you start, finish Module 5, the
other half of Gate 4, and Module 1,

39
00:02:23.435 --> 00:02:27.523
because the scope names service
principals by the enterprise

40
00:02:27.523 --> 00:02:31.340
application's object I D, as
the register records them.

41
00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:39.565
Unit 1 is the guide's Step 4,
sub-steps four d and four e.

42
00:02:39.565 --> 00:02:42.165
Four d is custom security attributes.

43
00:02:42.165 --> 00:02:47.172
You define an attribute set for applications,
such as environment and data class,

44
00:02:47.172 --> 00:02:51.881
and, as Microsoft Learn's page on
filtering for applications in Conditional

45
00:02:51.881 --> 00:02:56.290
Access says, a policy can then select
target applications by attribute.

46
00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.203
Only string attributes work.

47
00:02:58.203 --> 00:03:02.121
And because service principals
can't be members of dynamic groups,

48
00:03:02.121 --> 00:03:04.828
attributes are how this
track targets by fact.

49
00:03:04.828 --> 00:03:08.165
Four e is the workload
identity policies themselves.

50
00:03:08.165 --> 00:03:11.210
For each Tier 1 single-tenant
service principal,

51
00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:16.310
assign it directly to a location block that
allows only the networks it runs from,

52
00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:19.202
and to a high-risk block,
both report-only first.

53
00:03:19.202 --> 00:03:22.603
The results appear in the
service principal sign-in logs.

54
00:03:22.603 --> 00:03:25.211
Managed identities are
governed through their

55
00:03:25.211 --> 00:03:27.690
role assignments and
access reviews instead.

56
00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:32.119
And Microsoft Learn's Conditional
Access for workload identities page

57
00:03:32.119 --> 00:03:36.680
says creating or changing these
policies needs Workload I D Premium.

58
00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:43.017
Unit 2 is the context and the files.

59
00:03:43.017 --> 00:03:46.005
The program's design
guidance sets two policies.

60
00:03:46.005 --> 00:03:49.676
C A oh fifteen blocks the
ring's service principals from

61
00:03:49.676 --> 00:03:53.667
anywhere but one named location,
the workload egress ranges.

62
00:03:53.667 --> 00:03:56.955
C A oh thirty-seven blocks
them when their risk is high.

63
00:03:56.955 --> 00:03:58.455
Two decisions frame them.

64
00:03:58.455 --> 00:04:01.634
D N P four is licensing:
Workload I D Premium

65
00:04:01.634 --> 00:04:05.267
for the Tier 1 single-tenant
service principals.

66
00:04:05.267 --> 00:04:09.259
D N P five is enforcement, and
it's the CISO's call: enforce,

67
00:04:09.259 --> 00:04:14.380
extend report-only, or enforce with
exclusions that are dated and owned.

68
00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:19.252
The scope file is the record: each
in-scope principal by object I D and ring,

69
00:04:19.252 --> 00:04:24.368
and each identity that's out of scope,
with the control that covers it instead.

70
00:04:24.368 --> 00:04:27.093
And each policy is rendered once per ring,

71
00:04:27.093 --> 00:04:32.305
so enforcing one ring changes one policy
and leaves the next one in report-only.

72
00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:34.370
That's the program's design.

73
00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:42.049
The pre-merge check holds nine
rules, N P W C A oh one to oh nine,

